course: books
lesson: 22
title: Alice in Wonderland, chapter XI, part 2: Who Stole the Tarts? (the Hatter, the cook and a surprise witness)
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: In the middle of the trial Alice feels a curious sensation: she is growing again. She decides to stay as long as there is room. The Dormouse, squashed beside her, grumbles that she has no right to grow here; Alice answers that he is growing too, and he says huffily that he grows «at a reasonable pace», and moves away.
explain: The Queen has never stopped staring at the Hatter, and now she asks for «the list of the singers in the last concert». The Hatter trembles so much that his shoes fall off. His evidence is a muddle about bread-and-butter and «the twinkling of the tea», and the King keeps threatening to have him executed.
explain: When a guinea-pig cheers, the officers stuff it head first into a canvas bag and sit on it — that, Alice realises, is what newspapers mean by «suppressed». The King tells the Hatter he may «stand down»; the Hatter, kneeling, says he can't go any lower, so the King says he may «sit down». The Hatter escapes without his shoes, just before the Queen's order to take his head off outside.
explain: The next witness is the Duchess's cook, carrying her pepper-box, and everyone near the door starts sneezing. «Give your evidence.» — «Shan't.» Asked what tarts are made of, she says «Pepper, mostly», and a sleepy voice behind her says «Treacle» — the Dormouse, who is promptly thrown out of court. In the confusion, the cook disappears.
explain: The King, relieved, asks the Queen to question the next witness, as it makes his forehead ache. Alice is curious who it will be, since they have hardly any evidence yet. Then the White Rabbit reads out, at the top of his shrill little voice, the name: «Alice!»
fact: Alice begins «to grow larger again»; she first thinks of leaving the court, but decides to stay «as long as there was room for her».
fact: The Dormouse: «You've no right to grow here.» Alice: «you know you're growing too.» Dormouse: «Yes, but I grow at a reasonable pace, not in that ridiculous fashion.»
fact: The Queen asks for «the list of the singers in the last concert», and the Hatter trembles so that «he shook both his shoes off».
fact: The King: «Give your evidence, or I'll have you executed, whether you're nervous or not.»
fact: The Hatter mumbles about «the twinkling of the tea»; the King says «Of course twinkling begins with a T! Do you take me for a dunce?» (dunce = a stupid pupil).
fact: The Hatter says the March Hare said something; «I deny it!» says the March Hare; the King: «He denies it: leave out that part.» The Dormouse denies nothing, «being fast asleep».
fact: «I'm a poor man, your Majesty.» — «You're a very poor speaker.» The King plays on poor = having little money, and poor = bad.
fact: «Suppressed» means stopped or held down; here the officers put the cheering guinea-pig head first into a canvas bag and sit on it; later a second guinea-pig cheers and is suppressed too, and Alice thinks «Come, that finished the guinea-pigs!»
fact: «Stand down» means leave the witness box; the Hatter, already on one knee, says «I can't go no lower: I'm on the floor», so the King says «Then you may sit down».
fact: The Hatter leaves «without even waiting to put his shoes on»; the Queen adds «—and just take his head off outside», but he is out of sight before the officer reaches the door.
fact: The next witness is the Duchess's cook; Alice guesses who it is before she enters, because people near the door begin sneezing.
fact: To «cross-examine» means to question a witness closely; the King asks the cook «What are tarts made of?» and she answers «Pepper, mostly».
fact: A sleepy voice says «Treacle»; the Queen shrieks «Collar that Dormouse! Behead that Dormouse! Turn that Dormouse out of court! Suppress him! Pinch him! Off with his whiskers!» (collar = grab), and by the time the court settles, the cook has gone.
fact: The King says to the Queen: «you must cross-examine the next witness. It quite makes my forehead ache!»
fact: Alice thinks «they haven't got much evidence yet», and then the White Rabbit calls the next witness: «Alice!»
fact: Talk in this lesson: «ken» (Scotland) = know; «gurt» (West Country) = great, big; «yer man» (Ireland) = that fella; «hun» = love, dear; «can or not?» (Singapore English) = is it possible?
exercise: What curious sensation does Alice feel during the trial? | She is beginning to grow larger again | She is shrinking down to three inches | She is falling asleep | She is very cold
exercise: What does Alice decide to do about her growing? | Stay where she is as long as there is room | Leave the court at once and run all the way home to her sister and Dinah | Eat some mushroom | Hide under the table
exercise: Who is squeezed next to Alice and complains? | The Dormouse | The March Hare | Bill the Lizard | The Gryphon himself
exercise: «You've no right to grow here.» What does Alice answer? | «You know you're growing too» | «Sorry, I'll stop growing at once, I promise you» | «Mind your own business» | «Off with your head»
exercise: How does the Dormouse say he grows? | At a reasonable pace | Only when he sleeps, which is nearly all the time | Not at all | Faster than Alice
exercise: What does the Queen ask an officer to bring her? | The list of the singers in the last concert | The dish of tarts from the table, and a spoon | Her spectacles | The executioner
exercise: What happens to the Hatter when he hears this? | He trembles so much that he shakes both his shoes off | He starts singing «Twinkle, twinkle, little bat» to calm himself | He faints | He runs away at once
exercise: «The twinkling of the—what?» the King asks. What did the Hatter say? | The twinkling of the tea | The twinkling of the stars | The twinkling of the bat | The twinkling of the Queen's eye
exercise: «It began with the tea.» What does the King think he means? | That «twinkling» begins with a T | That tea began on the fourteenth of March | That the tea was stolen | That the tarts began it
exercise: «Do you take me for a dunce?» What is a dunce? | A stupid pupil | A dancer at a ball | A king's servant | A kind of tall hat
exercise: What does the March Hare say when the Hatter mentions him? | «I didn't!» and «I deny it!» | «Yes, I said it, and I'm proud of it» | Nothing | «Treacle»
exercise: «He denies it,» says the King. What order does he give? | «Leave out that part» | «Off with his head, at once» | «Write it down twice on your slates, jurymen» | «Ask the Dormouse»
exercise: Why doesn't the Dormouse deny anything? | He is fast asleep | He agrees with the Hatter | He is not in court today | He is shut up in a bag
exercise: «I'm a poor man,» says the Hatter. How does the King twist this? | «You're a very poor speaker» | «Then you may have a tart from the dish to take home» | «So am I» | «Then pay a fine»
exercise: What happens to the guinea-pig who cheers? | It is put head first into a canvas bag and sat upon | It is given a prize and a cup of hot tea for cheering so loudly | It is made a juror | It is sent home
exercise: What does Alice learn from the guinea-pig? | What newspapers mean by «immediately suppressed» | How to play croquet | How to grow at a reasonable pace, like the Dormouse | What tarts are made of
exercise: «You may stand down.» Why can't the Hatter go any lower? | He is already on the floor, on one knee | He is too tall and stiff to bend any lower than he is | His shoes are off | The Queen is watching
exercise: How does the Hatter leave the court? | In a hurry, without even putting his shoes on | Slowly and calmly, finishing his tea and bread-and-butter on the way out | In chains | Carried by soldiers
exercise: What does the Queen order as the Hatter leaves? | «Just take his head off outside» | «Bring him back for more questions» | «Give him a tart» | «Let him go»
exercise: Who is the next witness? | The Duchess's cook | The Duchess herself | The Cheshire Cat, grinning on a bough | The Gryphon, out of breath
exercise: How does Alice guess who the next witness is before she enters? | People near the door begin sneezing | She hears a frying-pan crash against the door outside | The cook shouts her name | The White Rabbit tells her
exercise: «Give your evidence,» says the King. What does the cook say? | «Shan't» | «More pepper!» | «Yes, your Majesty» | «Off with your head»
exercise: «What are tarts made of?» What does the cook answer? | «Pepper, mostly» | «Treacle, of course» | «Jam and pastry, mostly» | «Turtle soup, I think»
exercise: Who says «Treacle»? | The Dormouse, in a sleepy voice | The Hatter, from outside | The King, by mistake, reading from his note-book | Alice
exercise: What does the King ask the Queen to do with the next witness? | Cross-examine it herself, as it makes his forehead ache | Behead it at once, without asking any questions at all | Give it a tart | Send it home
exercise: Whose name does the White Rabbit read out last? | Alice's | The Knave's | The Duchess's | Bill the Lizard's
test: Ollie: «Alice shrank in court, tiny as a mouse.» Put it right. | She began to grow larger again, squashing the Dormouse | Right, she shrank to three inches tall and hid under the table | She stayed the same size | She turned into a pig
test: «I grow at a reasonable pace, not in that ridiculous fashion.» What is the Dormouse's point? | Everyone grows, but slowly — not suddenly like Alice | Dormice grow faster than girls, and that is quite reasonable | He never grows | He is jealous of Alice
test: Why does the Queen want the list of the singers in the last concert? | The Hatter sang there and she said he was «murdering the time» | She wants to invite them all to a new concert in the palace garden next week | She is bored | She wants to sing herself
test: Lucy: «the King was dead gentle with the Hatter, hun.» (hun = love, dear) Put it right. | He threatened again and again to have him executed | Right: he spoke softly and gave him a tart and a new pair of shoes | He ignored him | He cried
test: «He denies it: leave out that part.» What is wrong with this way of judging? | If anyone can just deny a thing, no evidence would ever count | It is the best and fairest way to find out the truth in any court of justice | The March Hare was asleep | Nothing at all
test: «I'm a poor man» — «You're a very poor speaker.» Which two meanings of «poor» does the King use? | Having little money, and bad at something | Sad and lonely, and very tired after a long day | Small, and thin | Rich, and kind
test: «If that's all you know about it, you may stand down.» — «I can't go no lower.» What is the joke? | «Stand down» means leave the witness box, but the Hatter takes it as get lower | The Hatter is standing on a chair and cannot climb down safely | The King is very short | The Hatter has no legs
test: Is this TRUE or FALSE? «The Hatter is beheaded outside the court.» | False: he's out of sight before the officer gets to the door | True: the officer catches him at the gate and does it at once | True, but he gets better | False: the Queen forgives him
test: Ryan from Bristol: «the cook gave gurt long evidence about tarts.» (gurt = great, big) Put it right. | She said «Shan't», then only «Pepper, mostly» | Right: she spoke for a whole hour about pastry, jam and how to bake | She said «Treacle» | She sang a song
test: Why does the court get into confusion? | The Queen orders the Dormouse thrown out, suppressed, pinched and his whiskers off | The Hatter comes back for his shoes and his teacup, and the Queen sees him | A hedgehog runs in | The tarts disappear
test: What would the King's trial be like if every witness disappeared like the cook? | It would have no evidence at all — just like now | It would find the thief in a minute, because nobody would argue | It would be finished properly | The Queen would be the witness
test: Looking back: where did Alice meet the cook before, and what was she doing? | In the Duchess's kitchen, throwing pans and plates and peppering the soup | At the tea-party, pouring the tea for the March Hare and the Hatter | At croquet, as an arch | In the White Rabbit's house
test: Looking back: in chapter IX, Alice invented a rule about pepper. Does the cook fit it? | Yes: pepper makes people hot-tempered, and the cook is rude and cross | No: the cook is very sweet and kind to everyone in the court | Yes: pepper makes people sleepy | No: the cook has no pepper
test: Looking back: why does «Treacle» make sense coming from the Dormouse? | His story at the tea-party was about sisters living on treacle | He works in a treacle factory near the March Hare's house | Treacle is his name | The cook told him to say it
test: Looking back: in chapter IV, Alice also grew too big for a place. Where? | In the White Rabbit's house, with an arm out of the window and a foot up the chimney | In the Duchess's smoky kitchen, by the fire | At the tea-table | In the jury-box
test: Looking back: in chapter IV, who held up Bill the Lizard and gave him something out of a bottle? | Two guinea-pigs | Two pink flamingoes from the croquet-ground | Two prickly hedgehogs | Two of the card soldiers
test: ★★★ Two guinea-pigs cheer and both are suppressed. Then Alice thinks «that finished the guinea-pigs!» What does she mean? | There are no more guinea-pigs free to cheer | All the guinea-pigs in the whole world are gone for ever | The guinea-pigs went home | The guinea-pigs were beheaded
test: ★★★ Careful: how many witnesses are called in this whole chapter, counting the last name read out? | 3: the Hatter, the cook, and Alice | 2: the Hatter and the cook | 4: the Hatter, the March Hare, the Dormouse and the cook | 1: the Hatter
test: ★★★ The cook says tarts are made of «Pepper, mostly» and the Dormouse says «Treacle». Who is closer to the truth about real tarts, and why? | The Dormouse: a tart is sweet, like treacle, not peppery | The cook: tarts are always full of pepper, as every cook in England knows | Neither: tarts are made of tea | Both equally
exam: Identify the occasion of the Hatter's trembling to the point that both his shoes were shaken off. | The Queen's demand for the list of singers at the last concert | The King's request, made in a loud voice, for a cup of tea and bread-and-butter | The entry of the cook into the court | The growth of Alice
exam: It is stated that a guinea-pig, having cheered, was «suppressed». Select the response which most accurately characterises the procedure. | It was placed head first in a canvas bag, upon which the officers sat | It was fined one shilling | It was expelled from the realm | It was made to apologise in writing
exam: Ascertain the evidence furnished by the cook in respect of the composition of tarts. | That they were made mostly of pepper | That they were made of treacle | That she declined to say | That they were made by the Knave
exam: Which of the following statements is consistent with the established facts concerning the third witness? | The White Rabbit called the name «Alice!» | The third witness was the Duchess | The Dormouse was recalled | No third witness was called
